"proof.txt (14149 bytes)\n     1→Proofs of the Obligations in obligations.txt — Natural Deduction\n     2→\n     3→1. PRELIMINARIES\n     4→\n     5→1.1 Notation\n     6→    e, e0, ...   expressions            n, m          integer literals\n     7→    value(e)     e is a literal         ->, ->*       as in semantics.txt\n     8→    [phi]        assumption phi         []            end of proof\n     9→    ND rules:    &I &E  (and intro/elim),  |I |E  (or intro/elim),\n    10→                 =>I =>E (implication),    ~I ~E  (negation),\n    11→                 forallI forallE,          existsI existsE,\n    12→                 RAA (reductio),           IND (structural induction),\n    13→                 |E with cited subproofs for each disjunct.\n    14→    Notation for formulas: & = and, | = or, ~ = not, => = implies.\n    15→\n    16→1.2 Closure lemmas for ->* (by definition of the reflexive-\n    17→    transitive closure; used without further comment)\n    18→    CL1  e ->* e                                  (reflexivity)\n    19→    CL2  e ->* f & f ->* g  =>  e ->* g           (transitivity)\n    20→    CL3  e -> f  =>  e ->* f                      (monotonicity)\n    21→\n    22→1.3 Background facts about Z (standard ordered-ring properties,\n    23→    used as premises)\n    24→    AF1  (trichotomy)          forall n in Z:  n < 0 | n = 0 | n > 0\n    25→    AF2  (irreflexivity)       forall n:  ~(n < n)\n    26→    AF3  (mixed transitivity)  a < b & b <= c  =>  a < c\n    27→    AF4  (negation reverses)   n < 0  =>  -n > 0\n    28→    AF5  (definition of >=)    n >= 0  <=>  n > 0 | n = 0\n    29→    AF6  (totality)            + and unary - are total functions on\n    30→                               Z with unique results; hence n1 + n2\n    31→                               and -n are literals of Expr.\n    32→\n    33→    Remark: the grammar of semantics.txt has no variables, so every\n    34→    expression is closed.\n    35→\n    36→2. LEMMA L1 (VALUE-IRREDUCIBILITY)\n    37→\n    38→    L1:  forall n. ~exists e'. n -> e'\n    39→\n    40→1. [n -> e']                                  Assumption\n    41→2. A derivation of n -> e' ends in a rule whose conclusion's left\n    42→   side matches the literal n. Left sides of the rules (sec. 3,\n    43→   semantics.txt): abs(e) [S-ABS], e1 + e2 [S-ADD-L], n + e2\n    44→   [S-ADD-R], n1 + n2 [R-ADD], abs(n) [R-ABS-POS/NEG]. None is a\n    45→   literal.                                   rule schemas\n    46→3. No rule instance has left side n; no derivation exists.   2\n    47→4. F                                          1, 3\n    48→5. ~exists e'. n -> e'                        1-4, ~I\n    49→6. forall n. ~exists e'. n -> e'              5, forallI\n    50→[]\n    51→\n    52→3. OBLIGATIONS O1, O2, O3\n    53→\n    54→O1a (side conditions well-formed)\n    55→1. The side conditions are \"n >= 0\", \"n < 0\", \"n3 = n1 + n2\":\n    56→   atomic formulas of first-order arithmetic with all variables\n    57→   ranging over Z.                            sec. 3, semantics.txt\n    58→2. Each is a well-formed proposition.         1, definitional\n    59→[]\n    60→\n    61→O1b (smallest rule-closed relation exists)\n    62→1. Let R = { S subset of Expr x Expr : S is closed under all rules }.\n    63→2. Expr x Expr is in R: for any rule instance with true side\n    64→   condition and premises in Expr x Expr, the conclusion is an\n    65→   element of Expr x Expr.                    trivially\n    66→3. R is nonempty.                             2\n    67→4. ->  :=  intersection of R.                 3, set comprehension\n    68→5. -> is closed under the rules: given premises in -> and a true\n    69→   side condition, the conclusion lies in every S in R (each S is\n    70→   closed), hence in the intersection.        4, definition of intersection\n    71→6. -> is the smallest such relation: any closed S satisfies\n    72→   intersection of R subset of S.             4, definition of intersection\n    73→7. -> exists and is smallest.                 2-6, existsI\n    74→[]  (O1 discharged by O1a and O1b.)\n    75→\n    76→O2 (CASE-COVERAGE):  forall n in Z. n >= 0 | n < 0\n    77→\n    78→1. [n in Z]                                   Assumption\n    79→2. n < 0 | n = 0 | n > 0                      AF1\n    80→3. Case n < 0:   n < 0                        right disjunct, |I\n    81→4. Case n = 0:   n >= 0                       2 (n = 0), AF5, =-substitution\n    82→5. Case n > 0:   n >= 0                       AF5\n    83→6. n >= 0 | n < 0                             2-5, |E\n    84→7. forall n in Z. n >= 0 | n < 0              1-6, forallI\n    85→[]\n    86→\n    87→O3 (CASE-DISJOINTNESS):  forall n. ~(n >= 0 & n < 0)\n    88→\n    89→1. [n >= 0 & n < 0]                           Assumption\n    90→2. n >= 0, i.e. 0 <= n                        &E 1\n    91→3. n < 0                                      &E 1\n    92→4. n < n                                      AF3 on 3, 2\n    93→5. ~(n < n)                                   AF2\n    94→6. F                                          4, 5, ~E\n    95→7. ~(n >= 0 & n < 0)                          1-6, ~I\n    96→8. forall n. ~(n >= 0 & n < 0)                7, forallI\n    97→[]\n    98→\n    99→4. LEMMAS L2, L3\n   100→\n   101→L2 (PROGRESS-EXPR):  forall e. value(e) | exists e'. e -> e'\n   102→    Proof by IND on e.\n   103→\n   104→Case e = n:\n   105→1. value(n)                                   def. of value\n   106→2. value(n) | exists e'. n -> e'              1, |I\n   107→\n   108→Case e = abs(e0), with IH: value(e0) | exists e'. e0 -> e':\n   109→1. Case value(e0), i.e. e0 = n:\n   110→   1.1 n >= 0 | n < 0                         O2\n   111→   1.2 Case n >= 0: abs(n) -> n               R-ABS-POS\n   112→       Case n < 0:  abs(n) -> -n              R-ABS-NEG\n   113→   1.3 exists e'. abs(e0) -> e'               1.2, |E, existsI\n   114→   1.4 disjunction                            1.3, |I\n   115→2. Case e0 -> e0':  abs(e0) -> abs(e0')       S-ABS\n   116→   existsI; |I\n   117→3. value(abs(e0)) | exists e'. abs(e0) -> e'  1-2, |E\n   118→\n   119→Case e = e1 + e2, with IH(e1), IH(e2):\n   120→1. Case e1 -> e1':  e1 + e2 -> e1' + e2       S-ADD-L; existsI; |I\n   121→2. Case value(e1), i.e. e1 = n1:\n   122→   2.1 Case e2 -> e2': n1 + e2 -> n1 + e2'    S-ADD-R; existsI; |I\n   123→   2.2 Case value(e2), i.e. e2 = n2:\n   124→       n1 + n2 -> n3, where n3 = n1 + n2      R-ADD, AF6 (totality)\n   125→       existsI; |I\n   126→3. disjunction                                1-2, |E\n   127→\n   128→All three cases give the disjunction; IND discharges the induction.\n   129→[]\n   130→\n   131→L3 (DETERMINISM-EXPR):  forall e a b. (e -> a & e -> b) => a = b\n   132→    Proof by IND on e, with case analysis on applicable rules.\n   133→\n   134→Case e = n:\n   135→1. [n -> a & n -> b]                          Assumption\n   136→2. F                                          1, L1 (no step from n)\n   137→3. a = b                                      2, RAA (vacuous)\n   138→\n   139→Case e = abs(e0), with IH for e0. Rules matching abs(_): S-ABS,\n   140→R-ABS-POS, R-ABS-NEG.\n   141→1. Case value(e0), i.e. e0 = n:\n   142→   1.1 S-ABS inapplicable: its premise n -> c contradicts L1.\n   143→   1.2 R-ABS-POS and R-ABS-NEG cannot both apply: their side\n   144→       conditions n >= 0 and n < 0 together contradict O3.\n   145→   1.3 Exactly one of R-ABS-POS, R-ABS-NEG applies (O2, 1.2), and\n   146→       its conclusion is fully determined (n, resp. -n). Hence\n   147→       a = b.\n   148→2. Case ~value(e0):\n   149→   2.1 R-ABS-POS/NEG inapplicable: their schemas require the\n   150→       argument to be a literal; e0 is not. (schema mismatch)\n   151→   2.2 Only S-ABS applies: a = abs(c), b = abs(d) with e0 -> c,\n   152→       e0 -> d.\n   153→   2.3 c = d                                  IH\n   154→   2.4 a = b                                  2.2, 2.3\n   155→\n   156→Case e = e1 + e2, with IH for e1, e2. Rules matching _ + _: S-ADD-L,\n   157→S-ADD-R, R-ADD.\n   158→1. Case ~value(e1): only S-ADD-L applies (S-ADD-R and R-ADD require\n   159→   e1 to be a literal). a = c + e2, b = d + e2 with e1 ->\n[output truncated: showing first ~2000 of ~4108 tokens]"